# Line endings for files whose bytes are asserted.
#
# There was no .gitattributes at all, so every checkout took whatever the
# platform's `core.autocrlf` decided. That is harmless for source — the
# compiler does not care — and fatal for a *fixture*, because a test comparing
# file contents byte-for-byte then passes on Linux and fails on Windows for a
# reason that never appears in the diff: the two strings render identically in
# an assertion message.
#
# Scoped deliberately rather than declaring `* text=auto eol=lf` for the whole
# repository. That would renormalise every tracked file in one commit, which is
# a large unreviewable diff and a merge hazard for anything in flight. Fixtures
# are the files that actually need the guarantee.
tests/fixtures/** text eol=lf

# The compliance mirrors and registers are read back and compared in tests, and
# they are also what somebody hands an assessor — a file whose line endings
# depend on who last wrote it is a poor artifact for that.
project_memory/operations/compliance/** text eol=lf

# The instruction-sync check hashes these byte-for-byte to decide whether a
# managed block in CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md and copilot-instructions.md is stale.
# Without a rule, checking out an old branch and coming back rewrites the line
# endings, the hash changes, and three instruction files are reported as drifted
# from a file whose content nobody touched.
project_memory/index/** text eol=lf
